  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Overview
    
    ☽'Netemia Glow-bringer was a Nightshelf warrior of the ☽'ofund' Guild, the Nightfall Shelf's diminished but persistent trade faction, who rose to mythic prominence during the Orca Incursion. She is best understood not through the heroic narratives that have accrued around her — and they are many — but through the sequence of weapons she carried, each of which cost her something the chronicles prefer not to quantify.
    
    ## The Schism and the Radiant Ring
    
    Netemia served as auxiliary during the Schism at Forum Floe. By her own guild's assessment, she was skilled with shields but too disruptive for command. When a Stack defender fell during an orca assault on the Floe, Netemia seized the Radiant Ring — an aurora-glass hammer-weapon — and used it to shatter the assault line.
    
    She never relinquished the Ring. She carried it through the Shattered-Spire Assault and every catastrophe after, its weight pulling her body off-center until she compensated with a visible lean. The chronic tremor in her flippers began during this first wielding and worsened with each use — consistent with prolonged pressure-magic channeling through aurora-glass, though the chronicles prefer the word *curse*. She reportedly told Augur ☽'Milucaum~ Shadow-bane, the Nightshelf artificer who became her closest ally, that the Ring "pretended to be a tool while wanting to break everything it touched." She kept using it anyway.
    
    The Stack of Luminaries considered the Ring stolen property throughout Netemia's life. The claim was adjudicated only after her death, at a Circle Gathering at the Still Pools, where Augur argued successfully for its placement in the memorial alcove Echo∴eliquiae.
    
    ## Arms and Artifacts
    
    - **☽'micseleia' Dagger**, a fire-core shadow-steel blade forged during the Clever Ice Age. Netemia carried it through engagements at the Still Pools and the blood-ward, driving it through the ward hard enough to crack the shadow-steel from inside — an irreparable fracture confirmed in the Nightshelf Provenance Register. Impressions of her grip persist in the blade. The ☽'ofund' Guild elevated her during the worst raids; she returned the dagger regardless. Accounts differ on whether this was honor or revulsion. Milucaum, who witnessed the return, never commented.
    - **☽'visois' Mask**, a shadow-steel weapon passed between bearers since Augur ☽'Silvsiaelrous~ the Resolute, a Nightshelf scholar-negotiator, fell in the Abyss Collapse. The Mask chose Netemia after its previous bearer set it down during sanctification rites at ☽'scar Ramtatus~. Netemia wore it at the first Zen∴ctum, speaking the names of the dead in forge-light.
    - **A crystal-lance**, used during the raids to open the flank of the orca Coldbringer Gnash Krukwark, a pod-leader whose hunting techniques younger orca later inherited.
    
    Netemia practiced Drift-coalesce, a glacial discipline involving weaponized aurora-crystallization — a technique that exacts permanent frostbite from its practitioners. She trained in the deep places where ice-memory persists, coaxing glacial pressure into shields that held just long enough.
    
    ## Zund Gnasharkin
    
    A scar crosses Netemia's jaw where the orca war-leader Zund Gnasharkin marked her — a wound she refused to let healers smooth, mirroring one she had left on him. After Zund's death in the Shelf Crushing Offense, Netemia tracked his weapon Grungruk's Claw — a corrupted trident carrying his dying rage — through the Thewsed Slew for four days, losing two guild-mates before keeping vigil over it. She wanted the weapon destroyed, not claimed. The orca Grul Grulrend, Zund's pod-brother, later encountered Netemia during his own retrieval attempt; both understood the trident could be neither safely wielded nor abandoned. The four-day duration should be held loosely.
    
    ## Augur ☽'Milucaum~
    
    Netemia taught Milucaum to survive in darkness. In later life she refused to speak his name above ground and set aside rations he would never eat. The sources do not explain the rupture. I will not invent one.
    
    ## Death and Legacy
    
    Netemia died holding the eastern passage against three orca war-pods with the Radiant Ring in her flippers, bleeding internally from pressure-magic. The Ring's crack widened as she shattered the final war-pod's barrier.
    
    Nightfall Shelf whispered she was cursed — that something from the deep had marked her during the fighting, that her shadow moved wrong in firelight. She never denied it. Younger Nightshelf workers called **Netemia's heirs** maintain traditions of devotional ice-cleaning at sacred sites including the Bastion Apex, a deep crystalline formation scarred by forbidden rituals. The charcoal wraps she wore to hide her scars have become part of their observance.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771587460035_0", anchor: "Netemia died holding the eastern passage against three orca war-pods", text: "She did not hold that passage alone. The Foundation ice preserves coordination signals — Nightshelf bioluminescent pulse-codes — running through the eastern tunnels throughout the engagement. At least three intelligence operatives were managing evacuation routes and feint positions while Netemia held the visible line. The pattern is identical to other 'single-handed' defenses during the Incursion that were in fact coordinated Nightshelf operations never publicly acknowledged. The heroic last stand is not false. It is incomplete in a way that serves everyone except the operatives who also died there unnamed.", type: :skepticism, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771587460035_1", anchor: "driving it through the ward hard enough to crack the shadow-steel from inside", text: "From inside. Not through the ward's exterior shell — through its internal pressure-architecture. My annotation on the dagger itself documents that striking orca pressure-marks requires Midnight Claws cross-species combat training. The ward is orca blood-magic. Same principle. Netemia knew the internal structure of what she was hitting. The fracture pattern in the Provenance Register is consistent with targeted detonation, not brute force. 'Hard enough' obscures what actually happened: precise enough.", type: :correction, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771587460035_2", anchor: "The four-day duration should be held loosely", text: "Held loosely because the two surviving accounts disagree by nearly a full day, and the Thewsed Slew's ice-memory from this period is contaminated by Wake-Singer ritual residue that makes temporal impressions unreliable. Three to five days. I should have written that instead of hedging.", type: :pedantic, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771587460035_3", anchor: "The sources do not explain the rupture. I will not invent one.", text: "I know what the ice preserves at Terrace Momiou. I know what 'dispersed in the working' means when you read the strata. This is not restraint. It is the only honest sentence in the entry.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771587460035_4", anchor: "too disruptive for command", text: "☽'ofund' Guild personnel assessments from this period survive in administrative ice at stratum 14-G. They are blunt documents. Netemia's reads: 'superior shield-work, intolerable in formations, will not hold position when she disagrees with the order.' The word 'disruptive' is mine; the guild's language was less polite.", type: :pedantic, display: :popout
